{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JiUeoK9E8DRi9y5CGafqPT","type":"tool_result","content":"260\t| ryu | Kaminari Ryu 2.0T | 1999 | 4WD turbo | 280 | 1,340 | 39,000 | Dealer |\n261\t| longhorn | Ironwood Longhorn 428 | 1969 | FR | 420 | 1,610 | 47,000 | Dealer |\n262\t| brandt32 | Brandt 3.2 CSR | 2000 | FR | 320 | 1,450 | 58,000 | Dealer |\n263\t| heck | Brandt Heck 3.6 | 1997 | RR | 360 | 1,380 | 92,000 | Dealer |\n264\t| libeccio | Vespucci Libeccio | 1995 | MR | 380 | 1,250 | 135,000 | Dealer |\n265\t| kiteCup | Tsubame Kite Cup Racer | 2000 | FF | 210 | 930 | Prize | Featherweight Trophy |\n266\t| ryuRally | Kaminari Ryu Rally Spec | 1999 | 4WD turbo | 330 | 1,230 | Prize | Red Clay Rally |\n267\t| works | Kaminari Ginga Works GT | 2000 | FR | 560 | 1,180 | Prize, or 310,000 after Licence A | Harbour Lights 200 |\n268\t| corsa | Vespucci Libeccio Corsa | 1998 | MR | 620 | 1,100 | Prize, or 520,000 after Licence A | Peninsula Touring Trophy |\n269\t| type9 | Halcyon Type 9 Prototype | 2001 | MR | 780 | 930 | Prize only | The Halcyon Grand Prix |\n270\t\n271\tMarque personalities, for text and badges: **Tsubame** (small, clever, swallow badge), **Kaminari** (thunder; serious Japanese-flavoured performance), **Castellan** (English; a shed, a lathe, a principle), **Brandt** (German-flavoured engineering, polite and terrifying), **Ironwood** (American; displacement as a moral position), **Vespucci** (Italian-flavoured; beautiful, a little dramatic), **Halcyon Works** (the Peninsula's own tiny race shop).\n272\t\n273\tStarting credits: **15,000**. All three used cars are affordable, and buying the Kite or Ginga leaves very little: that is the point.\n274\t\n275\tSelling returns 50% of the price. You can't sell your last car, so no save can get stuck. Prize cars can't be sold: \"Prize cars are not for sale. It is simply not done.\"\n276\t\n277\t### Tyres\n278\t\n279\t| Tyre | `tyreMu` | `alphaP` | dirt, gravel factor | Wear × | Price |\n280\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n281\t| Street | 0.95 | 0.14 | 0.45 | 0.8 | 600 (close to stock on Pico and Kite) |\n282\t| Sport | 1.00 | 0.125 | 0.50 | 1.0 | 1,200 |\n283\t| Semi-slick | 1.12 | 0.115 | 0.42 | 1.4 | 4,800 |\n284\t| Racing slick | 1.30 | 0.105 | 0.35 | 2.2 | 12,000 |\n285\t| Rally dirt | 0.84 | 0.17 | 0.72 | 1.2 | 2,500 |\n286\t\n287\tAppendix B's car rows are the stock setups: a car keeps its own `tyreMu`, `alphaP` and `tyreTable` until the player buys tyres. `tyreTable` maps `dirt` and `gravel` to the factor; other surfaces use `SURFACES`. Wear × goes in `wearX`.\n288\t\n289\t### Parts\n290\t\n291\tEach one changes numbers in `tunedParams`. \"Slider\" means a setting becomes available on the Setup tab once bought. Each visible result is listed.\n292\t\n293\t| Category | Part | Effect | Price |\n294\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n295\t| Engine | Sport exhaust | power ×1.04; louder, deeper engine sound and crackle on lift | 900 |\n296\t| Engine | Intake and ECU | power ×1.06 | 1,800 |\n297\t| Engine | Stage 1, 2, 3 (exclusive) | power ×1.10, ×1.20, ×1.35; `rpmPeak` +0, +200, +400; `redline` and `upshift` +0, +200, +500 | 3,000, 8,000, 20,000 |\n298\t| Engine | Turbo kit 1, 2 (exclusive) | Turbo cars: power ×1.15, ×1.35. Others: ×1.25, ×1.50 and `turbo: true`, `turboLag: 0.8`; turbo whistle and blow-off appear | 6,000, 15,000 |\n299\t| Weight | Stage 1, 2, 3 (exclusive) | mass ×0.95, ×0.90, ×0.85; `cgH` ×0.99, ×0.98, ×0.96 | 1,500, 4,500, 12,000 |\n300\t| Drivetrain | Clutch and flywheel | `shiftTime` 0.11 | 1,200 |\n301\t| Drivetrain | Race gearbox | `shiftTime` 0.08; slider: Gearing (`final` ×0.85 to ×1.15, labelled ACCELERATION to TOP SPEED) | 5,000 |\n302\t| Drivetrain | Limited-slip diff | slider: LSD (`lsd` 0.2 to 0.8) | 2,500 |\n303\t| Suspension | Sport | `loadK` 0.085, `cgH` ×0.96; slider: Balance (`rollF` 0.45 to 0.70, labelled TURNS IN to STABLE) | 2,000 |\n304\t| Suspension | Race | `loadK` 0.07, `cgH` ×0.92; sliders: Balance, Ride height (`cgH` a further ×1.00 to ×0.90; below ×0.94 kerbs jolt the body) | 6,000 |\n305\t| Brakes | Sport pads | `brakeG` +0.15 | 1,500 |\n306\t| Brakes | Race kit | `brakeG` +0.40; slider: Balance (`brakeF` 0.50 to 0.75) | 5,000 |\n307\t| Aero | Street kit | `clA` +0.15, `cdA` +0.02; a lip spoiler appears on the model | 2,500 |\n308\t| Aero | Big wing | `clA` +0.50, `cdA` +0.06; slider: Downforce (×0.5 to ×1.5); a large wing appears on the model | 6,000 |\n309\t| Tyres | see table | | |\n310\t| Body | Paint (any of the 16 palette colours) | Model recolours, shown under showroom lights | 500 |\n311\t| Body | Rims (4 styles) | Hub colour and spoke pattern change | 300 |\n312\t\n313\tDisplayed power and weight come from `tunedParams`. Event limits read those numbers. Engine sound follows `rpmPeak` and exhaust. Show a dyno pull (section 9) on buying any engine part: the before and after curve drawn in the HUD, with the engine sound rising.\n314\t\n315\t### Service Bay\n316\t\n317\t- **Oil change, 250 CR.** Each 40 km after the first 200 km since a change takes 0.5% of power, down to −8%. `c.oil` carries it. A fresh change gives a small bonus of +2% for the first 100 km. The mechanic says so in one line.\n318\t- **Car wash, 50 CR.** No performance effect (\"It is simply polite.\"). Mud builds up on dirt (`dirt` 0..1 on the car) and the wash removes it: see section 8.\n319\t\n320\t### Licences\n321\t\n322\tEach test is data: `{ id, title, carId, track, startS, startSpeed, standing, end, limits, assists, medals, brief, tip, demo }`. `limits` says what fails the attempt (off the road, a stop outside the box, recovery). Times run from the first movement, or from the start line for rolling tests. Compute `medals` with `tools/medals.mjs`: run the AI demonstration in the sim at `skill: 0.97`, time T, then gold = T×1.03, silver = T×1.09, bronze = T×1.18, rounded to 0.01 s, and freeze the numbers in the data. A test passes at bronze. A licence is won when all four tests have at least bronze.\n323\t\n324\t`Cn` below is corner index n of that track's list (`tr.cornerS[n]` is its apex).\n325\t\n326\t**Licence C, \"Provisional\".** Car: Kite (stock, street tyres). Track: Proving Ground. Reward: 2,000 CR. All gold: the Pico as a prize.\n327\t\n328\t| Id | Title | Section | Lesson |\n329\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n330\t| C-1 | Start and Stop | Standing, 400 m along the long straight, stop inside a 30 m white box | The brake is a pedal with a far end |\n331\t| C-2 | First Corner | Rolling 60 km/h, C0 −100 m to C1 +100 m | Turn in, apex, unwind |\n332\t| C-3 | Cone Weave | Rolling 50 km/h down the straight through 6 cones at 40 m spacing. A hit cone adds 1 s | Weight moves |\n333\t| C-4 | The Short Loop | C2 −50 m to C6 +50 m | Link corners, keep your head up |\n334\t\n335\t**Licence B, \"Club\".** Car: Ginga. Reward: 5,000 CR. All gold: Castellan Moth as a prize.\n336\t\n337\t| Id | Title | Section | Lesson |\n338\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n339\t| B-1 | Threshold | Proving straight, rolling 160 km/h, ABS off, stop inside a box 150 m on. Locking up is allowed and costs time | Squeeze, don't stamp |\n340\t| B-2 | Hairpin Exit | Kestrel, C5 −150 m to C7 +60 m. Traction control 0 | Feed the throttle |\n341\t| B-3 | Three Layouts | Proving C7 to C10, driven three times in Kite, Ginga, Ryu; times added | FF, FR, 4WD in the same corners |\n342\t| B-4 | One Lap of Kestrel | Kestrel, flying lap | Rhythm |\n343\t\n344\t**Licence A, \"National\".** Reward: 10,000 CR. All gold: 25,000 CR and a gold helmet on the trophy shelf.\n345\t\n346\t| Id | Title | Car | Section | Lesson |\n347\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n348\t| A-1 | Blind Crest | Brandt 3.2 | Cedar, C3 −100 m to C5 +100 m | Trust the road you remember |\n349\t| A-2 | Switchbacks | Moth | Cedar, C7 to C10, uphill | Momentum |\n350\t| A-3 | Red Clay | Ryu Rally | Red Clay, C7 to C10, dirt | Rotate with the handbrake |\n351\t| A-4 | Harbour at Night | Libeccio | Harbour Lights, flying lap, no margin for error | Everything, by lamplight |\n352\t\n353\tAfter each attempt: medal, time, gap to the next medal, then **Retry**, **Watch your replay**, **Demonstration** (the AI drives it, label `DEMONSTRATION`, examiner's voice over), **Back**. Three failures in a row: the examiner offers the Driving line for this test.\n354\t\n355\t### Events\n356\t\n357\tPoints per round: 10, 8, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1. Most points wins the series. Prize money is paid every round to the top four in the ratio 100%, 60%, 40%, 20%. The Bonus and prize car come only from winning the series. Each race advances the day counter.\n358\t\n359\t| Id | Event | Needs | Restriction | Rounds (track, laps) | AI pace | AI pool | 1st prize | Bonus |\n360\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n361\t| dawn | Dawn Patrol Cup | C | ≤210 PS | Kestrel 2; Harbour at dusk 2; Kestrel 3 | .86–.90 | Pico, Kite, Moth | 1,500 | 3,000 |\n362\t| fellow | Front-Drive Fellowship | C | FF, ≤230 PS | Cedar 2; Saltline 4 | .88–.91 | Kite, Kite (tuned), Pico (tuned) | 2,000 | 5,000 |\n363\t| feather | Featherweight Trophy | B | ≤1,100 kg | Cedar 3; Kestrel 3; Harbour at dusk 3 | .90–.93 | Moth, Kite Cup, Pico (tuned) | 3,000 | 8,000 + Kite Cup Racer |\n364\t| society | Rear-Drive Society | B | FR | Kestrel in rain 3; Saltline 5 | .91–.94 | Ginga, Brandt 3.2, Longhorn | 3,500 | 10,000 |\n365\t| clay | Red Clay Rally | B | Any; dirt tyres advised | Red Clay, 2 laps, 3 legs against one rival each | .91–.95 | Ryu, Ryu Rally | 4,000 | Ryu Rally Spec |\n366\t| touring | Peninsula Touring Trophy | A | ≤520 PS | Cedar 3; Harbour at night 3; Saltline 6; Kestrel 4 | .93–.96 | Brandt 3.2, Heck, Libeccio, Longhorn, Ryu (tuned) | 6,000 | 40,000 + Libeccio Corsa |\n367\t| h200 | Harbour Lights 200 | A | Any | Harbour, 10 laps, dusk into night, one stop needed, field of 8 | .92–.95 | Heck, Libeccio, Brandt 3.2 (tuned) | 20,000 | Ginga Works GT |\n368\t| gp | The Halcyon Grand Prix | A and Touring or H200 won | Any | Kestrel 5; Cedar 5; Saltline 8; Harbour at night 5 | .96–.99 | Works, Corsa, Libeccio (tuned) | 30,000 | Type 9, ending |\n369\t\n370\tTwenty-two races and twelve tests is about three hours for a new player. The ten-lap endurance needs exactly one stop at default rates: in testing, tyres fall to zero by lap 9 and the tank empties on lap 9 or 10, and lap time drifts from 117 s to 135 s. Short races use `wearRate` ×0.5.\n371\t\n372\t**Winning the GP:** a podium scene, the Type 9 reveal, then the credits, then the ending card, in the catalogue voice: \"You may keep driving. Most people do.\"\n373\t\n374\t### Arcade\n375\t\n376\tThe Arcade Gate on the map skips the career: **Quick Race** (any track, any car, laps, field size, Easy .85 / Normal .92 / Hard .98, hour, rain), **Time Trial** (any track and car, ghost of your best), **Couch Duel** (section 14). Every car and track is open here from the first minute, so someone trying the demo can see everything quickly. It pays no credits.\n377\t\n378\t### Home Garage and Museum\n379\t\n380\t**Home Garage:** your cars on a turntable with mileage, oil, wear and setup; choose your ride; sell; a trophy shelf with a 3D cup or medal for every win; your stats (days, km, wins, gold medals, % collected).\n381\t\n382\t**Museum:** all 14 cars with their brochure text and silhouette (unowned cars show as a dark silhouette until seen in a race), plus a page per track: map, length, corners, records.\n383\t\n384\t---\n385\t\n386\t## 7. Screens and controls\n387\t\n388\t### 10-foot rules\n389\t\n390\t- Art space is 480×270 (height stays 270; width follows the window, so lay out from anchors and check 360, 480 and 640 wide). One art pixel is 4 screen pixels at 1080p. Body text is the HUD font at scale 1 (28 px tall on a TV, the floor). Titles are scale 2 or 3. Never smaller.\n391\t- **One idea per screen.** At most 7 focusable items on a screen, at most 6 numbers in a spec panel, at most 4 lines of prose, at most 60 characters a line. If a screen needs more, make it paged or tabbed. No tables wider than a few columns, no scrolling walls.\n392\t- Focus is a thick animated box that slides to the next item with a tick sound. There is always exactly one focused thing.\n393\t- Every screen has a prompt row along the bottom showing what the buttons do right now. Same screens for every input. Back is always there.\n394\t- Menus move with the left stick or D-pad, with repeat; L1/R1 change tab. Keyboard: arrows or WASD, Enter, Esc, Q/E or Tab for tabs. Mouse: hover moves focus, left click confirms, right click goes back, wheel scrolls or nudges sliders, drag rotates cars in showrooms. Hide the cursor after 2 s idle and during races.\n395\t\n396\t### Prompts and glyphs\n397\t\n398\t`src/game/ui/glyphs.js` draws glyphs as `hud.sprite` rows, about 11×11 art pixels. PlayStation set: ✕ ○ □ △, L1 R1 L2 R2, Options, sticks, D-pad. Xbox set (Settings: Button glyphs): A B X Y, LB RB LT RT, Menu. Keyboard: keycap sprites with the key name. Mouse: left, right, wheel icons. `prompt(action, label)` picks by the last device used and redraws the moment the player switches. Default glyphs: PlayStation.\n399\t\n400\t### Controls\n401\t\n402\t| Action | DualSense | Keyboard | Mouse |\n403\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n404\t| Steer | Left stick | A/D or ←/→ | Optional mouse steering (Settings) |\n405\t| Throttle | R2 (analog) | W or ↑ | Left button with mouse steering |\n406\t| Brake, reverse | L2 (analog) | S or ↓ | Right button with mouse steering |\n407\t| Handbrake | □ | Space | |\n408\t| Shift up, down | R1, L1 | E, Q (also Left Shift, Left Ctrl) | |\n409\t| Camera | △ (tap) | C | |\n410\t| Look back | ○ (hold) | B (hold) | |\n411\t| Look around | Right stick | | Right drag |\n412\t| Recover | △ (hold 1 s) when off the road or stopped | R (hold 1 s) | |\n413\t| Pause | Options | Esc | |\n414\t| Confirm, back | ✕, ○ | Enter, Esc or Backspace | Left click, right click |\n415\t\n416\tAdd `Tab`, `F` keys and arrows to `input.preventKeys` as needed. While Create is held, ignore Options: Create + Options belongs to the hub. Vibration: `gamepad.vibrationActuator?.playEffect('dual-rumble', ...)` on kerbs, impacts, lock-ups and gear shifts, guarded, with a Settings toggle.\n417\t\n418\t### Flow\n419\t\n420\t```\n421\tTitle → (Continue | New Game) → Map\n422\tMap → Licence Centre | Event Hall | Dealer Row | Tuning Street | Service Bay | Home Garage | Museum | Theatre | Arcade Gate | Settings | About\n423\tEvent → round card → race → results → (replay) → next round → podium / prize\n424\tPause (Options/Esc): Resume, Restart, Retire, Assists, Settings, Handbook, Dev Mode\n425\t```\n426\t\n427\t### Screens\n428\t\n429\t| Screen | What it is |\n430\t| --- | --- |\n431\t| **Title** | The diorama at dusk, the title in pixel letters over it, a car driving past. `PRESS START`-style prompt for the last device. Continue, New Game, Settings, About. 10 s idle starts attract mode |\n432\t| **Map** | The peninsula diorama (section 8). Nine buildings. Stick or hover moves between them (the mouse picks with `camera.rayAt` and a ray-to-sphere test per building, and a click goes in), the camera glides, a card names the place with one line of text. A status strip: credits, day, licence, current car. A one-line nudge says what to try next |\n433\t| **Licence Centre** | The tower on the proving ground. Three licence cards, then four test tiles with medal icons. Test card: title, car, target times, the examiner's brief spoken in gibberish with subtitles |\n434\t| **Event Hall** | Event cards by licence tier, locked ones dimmed with the reason. Event card: restriction, rounds with mini-maps, prize. Warns if the current car doesn't qualify |\n435\t| **Dealer Row** | Used lot and three dealer showrooms. Turntable under lamps, slow orbit, attendant's voice. Card: name, year, layout, PS, kg, price, then the brochure lines on △. ✕ Buy, □ Test drive (one lap of Proving Ground), L1/R1 next showroom, left/right next car |\n436\t| **Tuning Street** | Eight tiles, four to a page: Engine, Weight, Drivetrain, Suspension, Brakes, Aero, Tyres, Body. In a tile, up to 5 parts, each with an effect in plain words and a before/after number. Setup tab: sliders for what you own, one line saying what each does. □ Test drive. A mechanic in the bay works and comments |\n437\t| **Service Bay** | Oil change and car wash, with short animations. Mechanic line |\n438\t| **Home Garage** | As in section 6 |\n439\t| **Museum** | As in section 6 |\n440\t| **Theatre** | Last race, saved replays, slots |\n441\t| **Arcade Gate** | Quick Race, Time Trial, Couch Duel as big tiles, then two or three simple picker screens, never one dense form |\n442\t| **Race HUD** | Top-left: position `P3/8` (scale 2), lap. Top-right: time, best lap, gap to next car. Bottom-right: speed (scale 3) and gear (scale 3) with a segmented rev bar and shift light. Bottom-left: mini-map dots (track polyline, cars in colour), tyre wear as four coloured squares, fuel bar when it matters, ABS/TCS/STB flashes when they act. Flags and messages centre-top. Keep the middle clear. Couch Duel uses a compact version for 240 wide |\n443\t| **Results** | One large table of at most 8 rows, then prize money counting up with a soft register sound, then points. Continue, Retry, Watch Replay, Save Replay |\n444\t| **Handbook** | Pages, one topic each: controls for your device, what you can and cannot do, assists, tyres and wear, pit stops, tuning in plain words, rally, medals. Reachable from pause and from the Map |\n445\t| **Settings** | Tabs. **Sound:** Master, Effects, Music (default 10%), Voices. **Picture:** Pixel size (fine or chunky), Look (native, plus `playstation`, `arcade`, `vhs_rental`, `no_filters`), Quality, Frame cap, Fullscreen. **Controls:** Button glyphs (PlayStation or Xbox), Vibration, Steering response, Mouse steering, Units (km/h or mph). **Assists:** defaults. Reset to defaults. About |\n446\t\n447\t### Touch-only devices\n448\t\n449\tAt boot, if `matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)')` is false and `(pointer: coarse)` is true, and no pad is connected and no key has been pressed, the title shows **CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED** and a gear button. A tap on the gear opens Settings, which accept taps on every item (tap = hover + confirm; a Back button appears). No on-screen controls, ever (`touch: false`). After 10 s attract mode runs; a tap returns to this screen. A key press or a connected pad lifts the gate at once.\n450\t\n451\t### Browsers without WebGPU\n452\t\n453\tThe engine reports `Failed to start`. Catch it and show a plain line: this game needs WebGPU (a recent Chrome or Edge, Safari 26, or Firefox 141 on Windows). In a hub session also call `AIHub.fail(message)`.\n454\t\n455\t---\n456\t\n457\t## 8. Art\n458\t\n459\t**Base:** the engine's native look: 3D low-poly, toon materials, the 16-colour palette (`ink plum red orange sand lime green teal navy blue sky cyan white mist slate night`), pixel outlines, 480×270 (`LOOK_PRESETS.none`). Tag cars and people with `setLookLayer(obj, 'actors')`. Build the race world from your own models and geometry; use the library for generic props (`oak`, `pine`, `birch`, `snowy-pine`, `boulders`, `rock`, `cactus`, `lamp-post`, `fence`, `crate`, `barrel`, `haystack`, `signpost`, `bench`). Packs aren't needed; if you use one, inline it as `__PIXEL_ASSETS__` data.\n460\t\n461\t### Cars\n462\t\n463\tUse the car kit (C2): a side-profile slab for the body, a narrower slab for the glass, a roof slab, sill, lamps, then per-car extras. Make the tail distinct on every car, because that's what the player sees: lamp shape and placement, spoiler, exhausts, width. Add:\n464\t\n465\t- **Wheels** as separate meshes: a steer pivot, then a spin group, tyre plus hub, with a visible spoke bar. Wheel positions from each car's own wheelbase and track.\n466\t- **Body motion** in a parent Group: pitch from `c.ax`, roll from `c.ay`, heave from `c.vy`; wheels stay on the road. This is the single best thing you can do for feel.\n467\t- **Lamps:** tail lamps `glow` in `orange`, and separate brake-lamp meshes in `red`, bright, visible only under braking. Headlights `glow` `sand`, on at dusk and night, with a `ctx.lights` request 12 m ahead for the player's car and a small red one behind.\n468\t- **Parts on the model:** lip spoiler, big wing, wheel styles and paint come from the install. Rally cars get mud flaps and a roof vent.\n469\t- **Mud:** a slightly larger shell of the lower body in `orange`, `toonMaterial(c, { dissolve: true })`, its `dissolve` tied to `1 − dirt`. The car wash tweens it back so the mud dissolves pixel by pixel.\n470\t- **Ghost:** the model with every mesh's material swapped for one clone of `toonMaterial(seatColour, { dissolve: true })`, `dissolve = 0.35`, no shadow, with a name tag above it in HUD text.\n471\t\n472\t### Tracks\n473\t\n474\t`src/game/render/trackmesh.js` builds from the `tr` record:\n475\t\n476\t- **Road:** quads per sample. Two close shades of the surface colour in alternating 8 m bands (the classic speed cue). Darker rubber on the racing line in braking zones (a shade darker where `speeds` falls). Edge lines, centre dashes on the pit straight, a start grid and a chequered start line. Kerbs alternate `red` and `white` every 4 m on the inside of corners (where `|kappa|` is high), about 1.6 m wide, slightly raised. Hard colour edges need unshared vertices.\n477\t- **Skirts:** ribbons from the road edge outward, 4 to 6 columns a side, out to 60–120 m. Height comes down from road level to a flat ground plane below the track's lowest point, plus noise, giving embankments and hills. Shorten a skirt where another part of the circuit is close (a coarse 20 m distance check at build time). Terrain colours in bands and blotches.\n478\t- **Barriers** at `±(width/2 + wallL/R)`: armco, tyre stacks, concrete, or catch fence per track, with `red`/`white` paint where the corner is hard.\n479\t- **Braking boards** at 300, 200, 100 m before major corners.\n480\t- **Skid marks:** a ring buffer of dark quads laid where `c.sliding || c.locked`.\n481\t- **Merging:** `mergeStaticMeshes` for the static world, as the guide says. Beyond that, performance is out of scope, so dress generously: crowds, flags, props, lights.\n482\t\n483\t### Track dressing\n484\t\n485\tEach has its own look and landmark. Build them from primitives and the library. These are the dressing lists:\n486\t\n487\t| Track | Hour | Look | Landmarks and props |\n488\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n489\t| **Kestrel Park** | 15:00 | Rolling English-style parkland, deep green, white fences | Grandstand and pit building on the straight, a windmill on the hill, oaks, hay bales, marshal posts with cloth flags, a tiny church. Kestrel bird flock (`Boids`) |\n490\t| **Cedar Ridge** | 10:00 | Mountain forest, thin mist particles | Pines by the thousand, rock cuts, a tunnel on the rise after C6 (box arch with lamps; darkens the sky while inside), a red steel viaduct overhead, a waterfall (`WaterSurface` plus particles), a hut and hikers |\n491\t| **Harbour Lights** | 21:00, or dusk 18:30 | Night city on the water, walls close | City blocks with lit windows (vertex colours and `glow`), neon signs in the game's own invented words, cranes and stacked containers, water along the front straight (`WaterSurface`), a Ferris wheel turning in the distance, street lamps with baked pools on the road, fireworks when the race is won |\n492\t| **Saltline Speedbowl** | 18:30 | Endless white salt at sunset | A huge banked bowl, tall grandstand, a flag tower, a blimp circling, flat mesas on the horizon. Dust devils at distance |\n493\t| **Red Clay Stage** | 17:00 | Orange canyon, low sun | Canyon walls (tall skirts), cacti, a rusted water tower, wooden spectator stands, arrows and warning boards before crests, dust trails that hang in the air |\n494\t| **Proving Ground** | 11:00 | An old airfield | Control tower (Licence Centre), hangars, painted boxes, cones, a windsock (`clothGrid`), the examiner's van |\n495\t\n496\t### Sky and night\n497\t\n498\t`applySky` gives a flat background, sun and ambient. Add depth: a far ring of silhouettes at 2 to 3 km per track (hills, mesas, skyline) in two or three flat palette colours, a sun or moon disc, a few chunky clouds. For night, raise `engine.ambient.intensity` until the road reads, then let baked lamp pools, `glow` windows and the headlight do the work. Raw `skyAt(21)` with no lights is close to black.\n499\t\n500\t### Diorama\n501\t\n502\tThe Map is a chunky island about 60×40 m, with a small road loop and tiny cars (the car models at 0.25 scale) driving around it. Buildings: Licence Centre (tower), Event Hall (mini grandstand), Dealer Row (three glass showrooms), Tuning Street (garage with sparks), Service Bay (spinning brushes), Home Garage (house with a roll-up door), Museum (columns), Theatre (marquee with lights), Arcade Gate (neon arch). Boats, gulls, clouds. **The sky slowly moves with your progress:** morning at the start, dusk by the National licence, night lights for the Grand Prix. Show the current car parked at Home.\n503\t\n504\t### Particles\n505\t\n506\tRegister in code, sizes whole art pixels, palette colours: tyre smoke (`mist`), dust (`sand`, `orange`), gravel spray, sparks (`sand`, `orange`), rain spray, exhaust pop (`orange`, `sand`), car-wash foam, confetti, fireworks. Taillight `Trail`s at night.\n507\t\n508\t### Screen flow\n509\t\n510\tTransitions with `engine.screen.cover('wipe' or 'blinds')` and `reveal`. `engine.screen.flash` on the green light. `engine.hitstop(0.05)` on a very hard wall hit only.\n511\t\n512\t---\n513\t\n514\t## 9. Animation\n515\t\n516\t**Base:** the engine's rig, `HERO_MODEL`, `HERO_RIG`, `ClipDef`, `compileClips`, and procedural tools (`Timeline`, `tweens`, `Squash`, `clothGrid`). Existing hero clips include `Idle`, `IdleLook`, `Walk`, `Run`, `Wave`, `Sit`, `SideFlip`. Recolour the hero by swapping mesh materials by name for overalls and race suits. Check new clips with `analyzeClip`.\n517\t\n518\t**New clips to author as `ClipDef` data:**\n519\t\n520\t- `PitJack`: crouch and pump a jack lever.\n521\t- `PitWheelGun`: kneel, gun to the hub, pull a wheel, fit a wheel, stand.\n522\t- `PitFuel`: hold a fuel hose to the flank.\n523\t- `MarshalFlag`: wave a flag overhead (`Wave` upper body, with the flag mesh parented to the hand).\n524\t- `ExaminerClipboard`: note something, look up, nod.\n525\t- `DealerPresent`: an open-handed sweep toward the car.\n526\t- `MechanicWipe`: wipe hands on a rag, look at the car.\n527\t- `PodiumCheer`: arms up, small jump; `PodiumBow`: a formal bow.\n528\t- `CrowdCheer`: a looping bob for instanced stand-ins.\n529\t\n530\t**Procedural and mechanical motion:**\n531\t\n532\t- Car body pitch, roll, heave; wheels steer and spin; engine shake at idle and on the limiter; exhaust pop.\n533\t- Turntable and slow orbit camera; a car sinking on its springs when \"bought\".\n534\t- **Dyno:** the car on rollers, wheels spinning, curve drawn live, exhaust shake.\n535\t- **Pit stop:** the crew runs in, jack lifts the car 0.15 m with a tween, wheels swap, crew runs off.\n536\t- **Car wash:** spinning brushes that travel the car, foam particles, mud dissolving.\n537\t- Start gantry lights, flags as `clothGrid`, the windsock, the windmill, the Ferris wheel, the blimp.\n538\t- **Podium:** a `Timeline` scene (shots, cues, subtitles), confetti, the winner's car rolling in.\n539\t- **Prize reveal:** dark set, one lamp comes on, then another, the car under a cover that slides away with a `Timeline`, fanfare, voice line.\n540\t\n541\t---\n542\t\n543\t## 10. Audio\n544\t\n545\tEverything is code. No audio files. All sound goes through one mix module.\n546\t\n547\t**Music volume defaults to 10%** on first launch and in every hub round (engine fact 3). Test it. The Settings slider moves in 5% steps. Engine, tyres and effects sit well above it.\n548\t\n549\t### Engine sound (the most important sound in the game)\n550\t\n551\t`src/game/audio/enginesynth.js` is a Web Audio graph built on any `BaseAudioContext`, so a test can render it offline:\n552\t\n553\t- Firing frequency `f0 = rpm / 60 × cylinders / 2`. Saw at `f0`, square at `f0 / 2`, triangle at `2 × f0`, filtered noise for exhaust, through a low-pass whose cutoff rises with throttle and rpm.\n554\t- Gain `0.35 + 0.65 × throttle`. Rev-limiter stutter at redline. Overrun crackle on lift with the sport exhaust.\n555\t- Per-car data: cylinders (3 for the Pico, 4, 6, V8 for the Longhorn, V10 for the Corsa, V12 for the Type 9), filter character, exhaust loudness.\n556\t- Turbo cars: a sine whistle rising with `spool`, and a filtered noise puff on lift.\n557\t- Connect to `audio.context.destination` once `audio.unlocked`, scaled by `audio.volume.master × sfx` every frame, respecting `muted`. Node has no context: guard it, and let tests read the synth's last rpm and throttle.\n558\t\n559\t### Other cars and the world\n560\t\n561\tOther cars: up to 3 nearest get `ctx.audio.loop` voices with `spatialize` and `pitch` from rpm. Also: tyre squeal (band-limited noise, gain from `use` and `sliding`), gravel and dirt rumble, kerb rumble (a square buzz at a rate from speed), wind (noise, gain from speed²), gear clunk, wall and car thumps (`layers`), brake lock chirp, handbrake ratchet, crowd swell near stands, rain, the pit crew's wheel gun, the lap chime, start lights beeps, and the final flag.\n562\t\n563\t**Menu sounds:** tick (focus), confirm, back, denied, the cash register for money, a soft camera whirr in showrooms.\n564\t\n565\t### Music\n566\t\n567\tEight original pieces as `Song` data (`bpm`, `tracks`, `patterns`, `order`), `square`, `triangle`, `saw`, `sine`, `noise` only. Write new melodies; never imitate the source's music. Style: warm, jazzy, unhurried in menus; propulsive in races. Use seventh and ninth chords in arpeggios or comping, syncopation, a walking bass, brushed noise drums.\n568\t\n569\t| Piece | Where | Feel | BPM | Key and harmony |\n570\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n571\t| Lounge | Title, Map | Bossa lounge, soft sine lead | 92 | D♭: ii7–V7–Imaj7–vi7 with a turnaround |\n572\t| Catalogue | Dealer Row, Museum | Vibraphone-like (sine, vibrato), very slow | 76 | Fmaj7–Em7–Dm7–G7 |\n573\t| Socket Set | Tuning, Service | Light funk, muted square | 108 | A minor pentatonic over Am7–D9 |\n574\t| Examination | Licence Centre | Sparse, metronomic, polite | 120 | C: bass pulse, three-note motif |\n575\t| Apex | Day races | Driving chiptune rock | 156 | E minor: i–VI–III–VII, a lead hook |\n576\t| City Lights | Night races | City-pop groove | 118 | B♭: Imaj7–iii7–vi7–IV |\n577\t| Red Clay | Rally | Twangy, galloping | 132 | G mixolydian |\n578\t| The Long Drive | Replay, credits | Calm arpeggios that open out | 84 | D: Imaj7–V/vi–vi7–IVmaj7 |\n579\t\n580\tPlus a short fanfare for medals and wins. Races start with music on by default, and there's a Settings option to drive with no music.\n581\t\n582\t### Voices\n583\t\n584\tAnimalese-style gibberish, written in code. `src/game/audio/voice.js`, `say(text, speaker)`: each letter of the text becomes a syllable of 65 to 85 ms, vowels pick formants through two band-pass filters over a saw (a 800/1150, e 400/2000, i 300/2400, o 450/800, u 325/700 Hz), consonants add a 12 ms noise click, the base pitch comes from the speaker, the sentence end falls, and `?` rises. A Voices slider in Settings. Subtitles always show the line in full. The same words always make the same sound, so characters seem to really be saying it.\n585\t\n586\t| Speaker | Pitch | Manner |\n587\t| --- | --- | --- |\n588\t| Ms. Arai, chief examiner | 210 Hz | Even, unhurried |\n589\t| Benno, mechanic | 130 Hz, square | Gravelly, short |\n590\t| Mr. Fenn, dealer | 180 Hz | A little lilt at line ends |\n591\t| Dell, race commentator | 160 Hz, faster | Excited, on replays and results |\n592\t\n593\t---\n594\t\n595\t## 11. Writing and cast\n596\t\n597\t**Voice.** The source's writing is a catalogue written by people in love with the subject: sincere, exact, formally polite, a touch translated, and funny only by being deadpan. Numbers appear with respect. Sentences are short. Nothing is \"awesome\". Nobody calls the player a champion. A car is never \"sick\". Keep to that here. All on-screen text is uppercase in the HUD font, so write it to be read that way.\n598\t\n599\t**Cast.** Only these four, each with a few lines per location. **Ms. Arai** at the Licence Centre has examined everyone on the Peninsula, and has never raised her voice. **Benno** at Tuning Street and the Service Bay and in the pits believes every car is one oil change from happiness. **Mr. Fenn** at Dealer Row can recite every car's kerb weight and still gets emotional. **Dell** does commentary: gibberish over replays, with subtitles of plain facts. They appear as the hero model, recoloured, in their set. No plot. The story is your car.\n600\t\n601\t### Brochure text\n602\t\n603\t| Car | Text |\n604\t| --- | --- |\n605\t| Pico | A city car of 660 cc, made to fit where other cars can only wish to go. At 720 kg it has little to hold back and little to push. It teaches the first lesson: momentum is expensive. |\n606\t| Kite | The Kite was a family hatchback until the engineers stayed late. The 1.6-litre engine asks to be revved, and the front tyres do all of the work. Brake before the corner and it will thank you. |\n607\t| Ginga | A front-engined, rear-drive coupe in good used condition. The steering speaks clearly and the rear axle answers the right foot. Many drivers learned to drive in this car. Some learned twice. |\n608\t| Moth | Two seats, one purpose, 860 kg. Castellan removed what the car did not need, then removed a little more. The power figure is modest. That is not the figure that matters. |\n609\t| Ryu | Turbocharged, four-wheel drive, born for a rally rule that no longer exists. Power arrives late and then all at once. In the rain, very little can keep it in sight. |\n610\t| Longhorn | A large American coupe of the old school: seven litres, 420 PS, and brakes that remember a simpler time. It does not like corners, and it says so. On a long straight nobody asks it to apologise. |\n611\t| Brandt 3.2 | A four-door sedan, according to the catalogue. The straight six reaches 7,900 rpm with an even temper, and the balance is close to 50:50. Businessmen bought it for the leather. Others did not. |\n612\t| Heck | The engine sits behind the rear axle, where Brandt has insisted for forty years that it belongs. Traction is wonderful. Lifting in mid-corner is not recommended. This is written in the manual. |\n613\t| Libeccio | Named for the south-west wind. A mid-engined V8 with a low nose and a high opinion of itself. Light on the front wheels at speed. Precise, if you are. |\n614\t| Kite Cup | A stripped Kite for a one-make series, with a roll cage where the rear seats used to be. 210 PS through the front wheels asks for a patient foot. |\n615\t| Ryu Rally | Works gravel specification: long travel, short gearing, and a bonnet that has seen weather. On dirt it is another species. On tarmac it seems to be looking for the exit. |\n616\t| Works GT | The Ginga as the factory would like to remember it. 560 PS, carbon panels, and a wing with its own opinion about corner speed. |\n617\t| Corsa | Homologated in the strictest sense of the word: Vespucci built twenty-five road cars so that it could race this one. 620 PS. 1,100 kg. Do not lift. |\n618\t| Type 9 | Built by a small works team on the Peninsula, in a shed, for one race. 780 PS, 930 kg, a closed cockpit and no radio. It stops as well as it goes, which takes some time to believe. |\n619\t\n620\t### Lines to set the tone\n621\t\n622\t- **Ms. Arai, first meeting:** \"Welcome to the Peninsula. A licence is not difficult to obtain. It is only difficult to deserve.\"\n623\t- **Ms. Arai, C-1 brief:** \"Accelerate to the marker, then bring the car to rest inside the white box. Please remember: the brake is also a steering wheel.\"\n624\t- **Ms. Arai, fail:** \"The box has not moved. Please try again.\"\n625\t- **Ms. Arai, gold:** \"Thank you. That was correct.\"\n626\t- **Mr. Fenn, on the Kite:** \"A previous owner. One careful weekend. She has 88,000 kilometres and no regrets.\"\n627\t- **Benno, oil:** \"Your oil is tired. It has seen things. Two hundred and fifty credits.\"\n628\t- **Benno, new wing:** \"You now have more downforce. You also have more drag. There is no third thing.\"\n629\t- **Dell on the results card:** \"Lap record at Kestrel Park. One minute, fifty-one. The windmill did not turn to look.\"\n630\t- **Loading tips:** \"BRAKE IN A STRAIGHT LINE. A TYRE HAS ONLY SO MUCH GRIP TO SHARE.\" \"SLOW IN, FAST OUT. THIS HAS BEEN TRUE FOR SOME TIME.\" \"A WASHED CAR IS NOT FASTER. IT IS SIMPLY POLITE.\"\n631\t- **Map blurbs:** Licence Centre, \"EVERY DRIVER BEGINS WITH A CLIPBOARD.\" Dealer Row, \"THREE SHOWROOMS AND A USED LOT. BRING MONEY.\" Tuning Street, \"WHAT YOU BUY HERE YOU WILL FEEL.\" Service Bay, \"OIL AND SOAP.\" Home Garage, \"EVERYTHING YOU HAVE EARNED.\" Event Hall, \"THE ROAD BETWEEN YOU AND THE GRAND PRIX.\" Museum, \"CARS YOU HAVE MET.\" Theatre, \"WATCH IT AGAIN, MORE SLOWLY.\" Arcade Gate, \"NO LICENCE REQUIRED. NO MONEY EITHER.\"\n632\t\n633\tWrite the rest in this voice: all test briefs and tips, event introductions, and part descriptions in one plain line each.\n634\t\n635\t---\n636\t\n637\t## 12. Attract mode\n638\t\n639\tAfter **10 s without input** on the title or the Map, start `src/game/attract/director.js`. Any input (key, click, pad, tap) stops it at once and returns to where the player was. It never runs in a hub session. It may cheat freely. It loops:\n640\t\n641\t| # | Clip | Shows | Seconds |\n642\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n643\t| 1 | Title card, `DEMO`, music | Identity | 3 |\n644\t| 2 | Showroom: three cars on the turntable, brochure text, Mr. Fenn | Cars, catalogue voice | 10 |\n645\t| 3 | Tuning: dyno pull with before and after curve, new wing appears | Tuning | 8 |\n646\t| 4 | Licence B-1 demonstration, medal awarded | Licences | 10 |\n647\t| 5 | Kestrel Park: AI race, starts in chase, cuts to TV cameras, a pass | Racing, replay cameras | 20 |\n648\t| 6 | Red Clay: the Ryu over a crest, dust, a handbrake hairpin | Rally | 12 |\n649\t| 7 | Harbour Lights at night, then a pit stop with the crew | Night, endurance | 20 |\n650\t| 8 | Saltline: slipstream at 300 km/h past the blimp | Speed | 10 |\n651\t| 9 | Podium, prize car reveal | Rewards | 8 |\n652\t| 10 | Credits card: `PRESS ANY BUTTON` | Call to action | 4 |\n653\t\n654\tEach race clip is a real `RaceScene` with AI on every car, `env` god settings, and hard cuts to skip dull parts. Since the player's profile isn't touched, nothing here can change the save. On a touch-only device it runs the same way.\n655\t\n656\t---\n657\t\n658\t## 13. Dev mode\n659\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"83ee8bb7-9f1f-4db7-994e-0a6a2ece78a9","uuid":"95796a26-76b5-4d79-abe3-5f792af5c28d","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:19:22.464Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-171910__x89353d/work/PLAN.md","content":"| ryu | Kaminari Ryu 2.0T | 1999 | 4WD turbo | 280 | 1,340 | 39,000 | Dealer |\n| longhorn | Ironwood Longhorn 428 | 1969 | FR | 420 | 1,610 | 47,000 | Dealer |\n| brandt32 | Brandt 3.2 CSR | 2000 | FR | 320 | 1,450 | 58,000 | Dealer |\n| heck | Brandt Heck 3.6 | 1997 | RR | 360 | 1,380 | 92,000 | Dealer |\n| libeccio | Vespucci Libeccio | 1995 | MR | 380 | 1,250 | 135,000 | Dealer |\n| kiteCup | Tsubame Kite Cup Racer | 2000 | FF | 210 | 930 | Prize | Featherweight Trophy |\n| ryuRally | Kaminari Ryu Rally Spec | 1999 | 4WD turbo | 330 | 1,230 | Prize | Red Clay Rally |\n| works | Kaminari Ginga Works GT | 2000 | FR | 560 | 1,180 | Prize, or 310,000 after Licence A | Harbour Lights 200 |\n| corsa | Vespucci Libeccio Corsa | 1998 | MR | 620 | 1,100 | Prize, or 520,000 after Licence A | Peninsula Touring Trophy |\n| type9 | Halcyon Type 9 Prototype | 2001 | MR | 780 | 930 | Prize only | The Halcyon Grand Prix |\n\nMarque personalities, for text and badges: **Tsubame** (small, clever, swallow badge), **Kaminari** (thunder; serious Japanese-flavoured performance), **Castellan** (English; a shed, a lathe, a principle), **Brandt** (German-flavoured engineering, polite and terrifying), **Ironwood** (American; displacement as a moral position), **Vespucci** (Italian-flavoured; beautiful, a little dramatic), **Halcyon Works** (the Peninsula's own tiny race shop).\n\nStarting credits: **15,000**. All three used cars are affordable, and buying the Kite or Ginga leaves very little: that is the point.\n\nSelling returns 50% of the price. You can't sell your last car, so no save can get stuck. Prize cars can't be sold: \"Prize cars are not for sale. It is simply not done.\"\n\n### Tyres\n\n| Tyre | `tyreMu` | `alphaP` | dirt, gravel factor | Wear × | Price |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Street | 0.95 | 0.14 | 0.45 | 0.8 | 600 (close to stock on Pico and Kite) |\n| Sport | 1.00 | 0.125 | 0.50 | 1.0 | 1,200 |\n| Semi-slick | 1.12 | 0.115 | 0.42 | 1.4 | 4,800 |\n| Racing slick | 1.30 | 0.105 | 0.35 | 2.2 | 12,000 |\n| Rally dirt | 0.84 | 0.17 | 0.72 | 1.2 | 2,500 |\n\nAppendix B's car rows are the stock setups: a car keeps its own `tyreMu`, `alphaP` and `tyreTable` until the player buys tyres. `tyreTable` maps `dirt` and `gravel` to the factor; other surfaces use `SURFACES`. Wear × goes in `wearX`.\n\n### Parts\n\nEach one changes numbers in `tunedParams`. \"Slider\" means a setting becomes available on the Setup tab once bought. Each visible result is listed.\n\n| Category | Part | Effect | Price |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Engine | Sport exhaust | power ×1.04; louder, deeper engine sound and crackle on lift | 900 |\n| Engine | Intake and ECU | power ×1.06 | 1,800 |\n| Engine | Stage 1, 2, 3 (exclusive) | power ×1.10, ×1.20, ×1.35; `rpmPeak` +0, +200, +400; `redline` and `upshift` +0, +200, +500 | 3,000, 8,000, 20,000 |\n| Engine | Turbo kit 1, 2 (exclusive) | Turbo cars: power ×1.15, ×1.35. Others: ×1.25, ×1.50 and `turbo: true`, `turboLag: 0.8`; turbo whistle and blow-off appear | 6,000, 15,000 |\n| Weight | Stage 1, 2, 3 (exclusive) | mass ×0.95, ×0.90, ×0.85; `cgH` ×0.99, ×0.98, ×0.96 | 1,500, 4,500, 12,000 |\n| Drivetrain | Clutch and flywheel | `shiftTime` 0.11 | 1,200 |\n| Drivetrain | Race gearbox | `shiftTime` 0.08; slider: Gearing (`final` ×0.85 to ×1.15, labelled ACCELERATION to TOP SPEED) | 5,000 |\n| Drivetrain | Limited-slip diff | slider: LSD (`lsd` 0.2 to 0.8) | 2,500 |\n| Suspension | Sport | `loadK` 0.085, `cgH` ×0.96; slider: Balance (`rollF` 0.45 to 0.70, labelled TURNS IN to STABLE) | 2,000 |\n| Suspension | Race | `loadK` 0.07, `cgH` ×0.92; sliders: Balance, Ride height (`cgH` a further ×1.00 to ×0.90; below ×0.94 kerbs jolt the body) | 6,000 |\n| Brakes | Sport pads | `brakeG` +0.15 | 1,500 |\n| Brakes | Race kit | `brakeG` +0.40; slider: Balance (`brakeF` 0.50 to 0.75) | 5,000 |\n| Aero | Street kit | `clA` +0.15, `cdA` +0.02; a lip spoiler appears on the model | 2,500 |\n| Aero | Big wing | `clA` +0.50, `cdA` +0.06; slider: Downforce (×0.5 to ×1.5); a large wing appears on the model | 6,000 |\n| Tyres | see table | | |\n| Body | Paint (any of the 16 palette colours) | Model recolours, shown under showroom lights | 500 |\n| Body | Rims (4 styles) | Hub colour and spoke pattern change | 300 |\n\nDisplayed power and weight come from `tunedParams`. Event limits read those numbers. Engine sound follows `rpmPeak` and exhaust. Show a dyno pull (section 9) on buying any engine part: the before and after curve drawn in the HUD, with the engine sound rising.\n\n### Service Bay\n\n- **Oil change, 250 CR.** Each 40 km after the first 200 km since a change takes 0.5% of power, down to −8%. `c.oil` carries it. A fresh change gives a small bonus of +2% for the first 100 km. The mechanic says so in one line.\n- **Car wash, 50 CR.** No performance effect (\"It is simply polite.\"). Mud builds up on dirt (`dirt` 0..1 on the car) and the wash removes it: see section 8.\n\n### Licences\n\nEach test is data: `{ id, title, carId, track, startS, startSpeed, standing, end, limits, assists, medals, brief, tip, demo }`. `limits` says what fails the attempt (off the road, a stop outside the box, recovery). Times run from the first movement, or from the start line for rolling tests. Compute `medals` with `tools/medals.mjs`: run the AI demonstration in the sim at `skill: 0.97`, time T, then gold = T×1.03, silver = T×1.09, bronze = T×1.18, rounded to 0.01 s, and freeze the numbers in the data. A test passes at bronze. A licence is won when all four tests have at least bronze.\n\n`Cn` below is corner index n of that track's list (`tr.cornerS[n]` is its apex).\n\n**Licence C, \"Provisional\".** Car: Kite (stock, street tyres). Track: Proving Ground. Reward: 2,000 CR. All gold: the Pico as a prize.\n\n| Id | Title | Section | Lesson |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| C-1 | Start and Stop | Standing, 400 m along the long straight, stop inside a 30 m white box | The brake is a pedal with a far end |\n| C-2 | First Corner | Rolling 60 km/h, C0 −100 m to C1 +100 m | Turn in, apex, unwind |\n| C-3 | Cone Weave | Rolling 50 km/h down the straight through 6 cones at 40 m spacing. A hit cone adds 1 s | Weight moves |\n| C-4 | The Short Loop | C2 −50 m to C6 +50 m | Link corners, keep your head up |\n\n**Licence B, \"Club\".** Car: Ginga. Reward: 5,000 CR. All gold: Castellan Moth as a prize.\n\n| Id | Title | Section | Lesson |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| B-1 | Threshold | Proving straight, rolling 160 km/h, ABS off, stop inside a box 150 m on. Locking up is allowed and costs time | Squeeze, don't stamp |\n| B-2 | Hairpin Exit | Kestrel, C5 −150 m to C7 +60 m. Traction control 0 | Feed the throttle |\n| B-3 | Three Layouts | Proving C7 to C10, driven three times in Kite, Ginga, Ryu; times added | FF, FR, 4WD in the same corners |\n| B-4 | One Lap of Kestrel | Kestrel, flying lap | Rhythm |\n\n**Licence A, \"National\".** Reward: 10,000 CR. All gold: 25,000 CR and a gold helmet on the trophy shelf.\n\n| Id | Title | Car | Section | Lesson |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| A-1 | Blind Crest | Brandt 3.2 | Cedar, C3 −100 m to C5 +100 m | Trust the road you remember |\n| A-2 | Switchbacks | Moth | Cedar, C7 to C10, uphill | Momentum |\n| A-3 | Red Clay | Ryu Rally | Red Clay, C7 to C10, dirt | Rotate with the handbrake |\n| A-4 | Harbour at Night | Libeccio | Harbour Lights, flying lap, no margin for error | Everything, by lamplight |\n\nAfter each attempt: medal, time, gap to the next medal, then **Retry**, **Watch your replay**, **Demonstration** (the AI drives it, label `DEMONSTRATION`, examiner's voice over), **Back**. Three failures in a row: the examiner offers the Driving line for this test.\n\n### Events\n\nPoints per round: 10, 8, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1. Most points wins the series. Prize money is paid every round to the top four in the ratio 100%, 60%, 40%, 20%. The Bonus and prize car come only from winning the series. Each race advances the day counter.\n\n| Id | Event | Needs | Restriction | Rounds (track, laps) | AI pace | AI pool | 1st prize | Bonus |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| dawn | Dawn Patrol Cup | C | ≤210 PS | Kestrel 2; Harbour at dusk 2; Kestrel 3 | .86–.90 | Pico, Kite, Moth | 1,500 | 3,000 |\n| fellow | Front-Drive Fellowship | C | FF, ≤230 PS | Cedar 2; Saltline 4 | .88–.91 | Kite, Kite (tuned), Pico (tuned) | 2,000 | 5,000 |\n| feather | Featherweight Trophy | B | ≤1,100 kg | Cedar 3; Kestrel 3; Harbour at dusk 3 | .90–.93 | Moth, Kite Cup, Pico (tuned) | 3,000 | 8,000 + Kite Cup Racer |\n| society | Rear-Drive Society | B | FR | Kestrel in rain 3; Saltline 5 | .91–.94 | Ginga, Brandt 3.2, Longhorn | 3,500 | 10,000 |\n| clay | Red Clay Rally | B | Any; dirt tyres advised | Red Clay, 2 laps, 3 legs against one rival each | .91–.95 | Ryu, Ryu Rally | 4,000 | Ryu Rally Spec |\n| touring | Peninsula Touring Trophy | A | ≤520 PS | Cedar 3; Harbour at night 3; Saltline 6; Kestrel 4 | .93–.96 | Brandt 3.2, Heck, Libeccio, Longhorn, Ryu (tuned) | 6,000 | 40,000 + Libeccio Corsa |\n| h200 | Harbour Lights 200 | A | Any | Harbour, 10 laps, dusk into night, one stop needed, field of 8 | .92–.95 | Heck, Libeccio, Brandt 3.2 (tuned) | 20,000 | Ginga Works GT |\n| gp | The Halcyon Grand Prix | A and Touring or H200 won | Any | Kestrel 5; Cedar 5; Saltline 8; Harbour at night 5 | .96–.99 | Works, Corsa, Libeccio (tuned) | 30,000 | Type 9, ending |\n\nTwenty-two races and twelve tests is about three hours for a new player. The ten-lap endurance needs exactly one stop at default rates: in testing, tyres fall to zero by lap 9 and the tank empties on lap 9 or 10, and lap time drifts from 117 s to 135 s. Short races use `wearRate` ×0.5.\n\n**Winning the GP:** a podium scene, the Type 9 reveal, then the credits, then the ending card, in the catalogue voice: \"You may keep driving. Most people do.\"\n\n### Arcade\n\nThe Arcade Gate on the map skips the career: **Quick Race** (any track, any car, laps, field size, Easy .85 / Normal .92 / Hard .98, hour, rain), **Time Trial** (any track and car, ghost of your best), **Couch Duel** (section 14). Every car and track is open here from the first minute, so someone trying the demo can see everything quickly. It pays no credits.\n\n### Home Garage and Museum\n\n**Home Garage:** your cars on a turntable with mileage, oil, wear and setup; choose your ride; sell; a trophy shelf with a 3D cup or medal for every win; your stats (days, km, wins, gold medals, % collected).\n\n**Museum:** all 14 cars with their brochure text and silhouette (unowned cars show as a dark silhouette until seen in a race), plus a page per track: map, length, corners, records.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Screens and controls\n\n### 10-foot rules\n\n- Art space is 480×270 (height stays 270; width follows the window, so lay out from anchors and check 360, 480 and 640 wide). One art pixel is 4 screen pixels at 1080p. Body text is the HUD font at scale 1 (28 px tall on a TV, the floor). Titles are scale 2 or 3. Never smaller.\n- **One idea per screen.** At most 7 focusable items on a screen, at most 6 numbers in a spec panel, at most 4 lines of prose, at most 60 characters a line. If a screen needs more, make it paged or tabbed. No tables wider than a few columns, no scrolling walls.\n- Focus is a thick animated box that slides to the next item with a tick sound. There is always exactly one focused thing.\n- Every screen has a prompt row along the bottom showing what the buttons do right now. Same screens for every input. Back is always there.\n- Menus move with the left stick or D-pad, with repeat; L1/R1 change tab. Keyboard: arrows or WASD, Enter, Esc, Q/E or Tab for tabs. Mouse: hover moves focus, left click confirms, right click goes back, wheel scrolls or nudges sliders, drag rotates cars in showrooms. Hide the cursor after 2 s idle and during races.\n\n### Prompts and glyphs\n\n`src/game/ui/glyphs.js` draws glyphs as `hud.sprite` rows, about 11×11 art pixels. PlayStation set: ✕ ○ □ △, L1 R1 L2 R2, Options, sticks, D-pad. Xbox set (Settings: Button glyphs): A B X Y, LB RB LT RT, Menu. Keyboard: keycap sprites with the key name. Mouse: left, right, wheel icons. `prompt(action, label)` picks by the last device used and redraws the moment the player switches. Default glyphs: PlayStation.\n\n### Controls\n\n| Action | DualSense | Keyboard | Mouse |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Steer | Left stick | A/D or ←/→ | Optional mouse steering (Settings) |\n| Throttle | R2 (analog) | W or ↑ | Left button with mouse steering |\n| Brake, reverse | L2 (analog) | S or ↓ | Right button with mouse steering |\n| Handbrake | □ | Space | |\n| Shift up, down | R1, L1 | E, Q (also Left Shift, Left Ctrl) | |\n| Camera | △ (tap) | C | |\n| Look back | ○ (hold) | B (hold) | |\n| Look around | Right stick | | Right drag |\n| Recover | △ (hold 1 s) when off the road or stopped | R (hold 1 s) | |\n| Pause | Options | Esc | |\n| Confirm, back | ✕, ○ | Enter, Esc or Backspace | Left click, right click |\n\nAdd `Tab`, `F` keys and arrows to `input.preventKeys` as needed. While Create is held, ignore Options: Create + Options belongs to the hub. Vibration: `gamepad.vibrationActuator?.playEffect('dual-rumble', ...)` on kerbs, impacts, lock-ups and gear shifts, guarded, with a Settings toggle.\n\n### Flow\n\n```\nTitle → (Continue | New Game) → Map\nMap → Licence Centre | Event Hall | Dealer Row | Tuning Street | Service Bay | Home Garage | Museum | Theatre | Arcade Gate | Settings | About\nEvent → round card → race → results → (replay) → next round → podium / prize\nPause (Options/Esc): Resume, Restart, Retire, Assists, Settings, Handbook, Dev Mode\n```\n\n### Screens\n\n| Screen | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Title** | The diorama at dusk, the title in pixel letters over it, a car driving past. `PRESS START`-style prompt for the last device. Continue, New Game, Settings, About. 10 s idle starts attract mode |\n| **Map** | The peninsula diorama (section 8). Nine buildings. Stick or hover moves between them (the mouse picks with `camera.rayAt` and a ray-to-sphere test per building, and a click goes in), the camera glides, a card names the place with one line of text. A status strip: credits, day, licence, current car. A one-line nudge says what to try next |\n| **Licence Centre** | The tower on the proving ground. Three licence cards, then four test tiles with medal icons. Test card: title, car, target times, the examiner's brief spoken in gibberish with subtitles |\n| **Event Hall** | Event cards by licence tier, locked ones dimmed with the reason. Event card: restriction, rounds with mini-maps, prize. Warns if the current car doesn't qualify |\n| **Dealer Row** | Used lot and three dealer showrooms. Turntable under lamps, slow orbit, attendant's voice. Card: name, year, layout, PS, kg, price, then the brochure lines on △. ✕ Buy, □ Test drive (one lap of Proving Ground), L1/R1 next showroom, left/right next car |\n| **Tuning Street** | Eight tiles, four to a page: Engine, Weight, Drivetrain, Suspension, Brakes, Aero, Tyres, Body. In a tile, up to 5 parts, each with an effect in plain words and a before/after number. Setup tab: sliders for what you own, one line saying what each does. □ Test drive. A mechanic in the bay works and comments |\n| **Service Bay** | Oil change and car wash, with short animations. Mechanic line |\n| **Home Garage** | As in section 6 |\n| **Museum** | As in section 6 |\n| **Theatre** | Last race, saved replays, slots |\n| **Arcade Gate** | Quick Race, Time Trial, Couch Duel as big tiles, then two or three simple picker screens, never one dense form |\n| **Race HUD** | Top-left: position `P3/8` (scale 2), lap. Top-right: time, best lap, gap to next car. Bottom-right: speed (scale 3) and gear (scale 3) with a segmented rev bar and shift light. Bottom-left: mini-map dots (track polyline, cars in colour), tyre wear as four coloured squares, fuel bar when it matters, ABS/TCS/STB flashes when they act. Flags and messages centre-top. Keep the middle clear. Couch Duel uses a compact version for 240 wide |\n| **Results** | One large table of at most 8 rows, then prize money counting up with a soft register sound, then points. Continue, Retry, Watch Replay, Save Replay |\n| **Handbook** | Pages, one topic each: controls for your device, what you can and cannot do, assists, tyres and wear, pit stops, tuning in plain words, rally, medals. Reachable from pause and from the Map |\n| **Settings** | Tabs. **Sound:** Master, Effects, Music (default 10%), Voices. **Picture:** Pixel size (fine or chunky), Look (native, plus `playstation`, `arcade`, `vhs_rental`, `no_filters`), Quality, Frame cap, Fullscreen. **Controls:** Button glyphs (PlayStation or Xbox), Vibration, Steering response, Mouse steering, Units (km/h or mph). **Assists:** defaults. Reset to defaults. About |\n\n### Touch-only devices\n\nAt boot, if `matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)')` is false and `(pointer: coarse)` is true, and no pad is connected and no key has been pressed, the title shows **CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED** and a gear button. A tap on the gear opens Settings, which accept taps on every item (tap = hover + confirm; a Back button appears). No on-screen controls, ever (`touch: false`). After 10 s attract mode runs; a tap returns to this screen. A key press or a connected pad lifts the gate at once.\n\n### Browsers without WebGPU\n\nThe engine reports `Failed to start`. Catch it and show a plain line: this game needs WebGPU (a recent Chrome or Edge, Safari 26, or Firefox 141 on Windows). In a hub session also call `AIHub.fail(message)`.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Art\n\n**Base:** the engine's native look: 3D low-poly, toon materials, the 16-colour palette (`ink plum red orange sand lime green teal navy blue sky cyan white mist slate night`), pixel outlines, 480×270 (`LOOK_PRESETS.none`). Tag cars and people with `setLookLayer(obj, 'actors')`. Build the race world from your own models and geometry; use the library for generic props (`oak`, `pine`, `birch`, `snowy-pine`, `boulders`, `rock`, `cactus`, `lamp-post`, `fence`, `crate`, `barrel`, `haystack`, `signpost`, `bench`). Packs aren't needed; if you use one, inline it as `__PIXEL_ASSETS__` data.\n\n### Cars\n\nUse the car kit (C2): a side-profile slab for the body, a narrower slab for the glass, a roof slab, sill, lamps, then per-car extras. Make the tail distinct on every car, because that's what the player sees: lamp shape and placement, spoiler, exhausts, width. Add:\n\n- **Wheels** as separate meshes: a steer pivot, then a spin group, tyre plus hub, with a visible spoke bar. Wheel positions from each car's own wheelbase and track.\n- **Body motion** in a parent Group: pitch from `c.ax`, roll from `c.ay`, heave from `c.vy`; wheels stay on the road. This is the single best thing you can do for feel.\n- **Lamps:** tail lamps `glow` in `orange`, and separate brake-lamp meshes in `red`, bright, visible only under braking. Headlights `glow` `sand`, on at dusk and night, with a `ctx.lights` request 12 m ahead for the player's car and a small red one behind.\n- **Parts on the model:** lip spoiler, big wing, wheel styles and paint come from the install. Rally cars get mud flaps and a roof vent.\n- **Mud:** a slightly larger shell of the lower body in `orange`, `toonMaterial(c, { dissolve: true })`, its `dissolve` tied to `1 − dirt`. The car wash tweens it back so the mud dissolves pixel by pixel.\n- **Ghost:** the model with every mesh's material swapped for one clone of `toonMaterial(seatColour, { dissolve: true })`, `dissolve = 0.35`, no shadow, with a name tag above it in HUD text.\n\n### Tracks\n\n`src/game/render/trackmesh.js` builds from the `tr` record:\n\n- **Road:** quads per sample. Two close shades of the surface colour in alternating 8 m bands (the classic speed cue). Darker rubber on the racing line in braking zones (a shade darker where `speeds` falls). Edge lines, centre dashes on the pit straight, a start grid and a chequered start line. Kerbs alternate `red` and `white` every 4 m on the inside of corners (where `|kappa|` is high), about 1.6 m wide, slightly raised. Hard colour edges need unshared vertices.\n- **Skirts:** ribbons from the road edge outward, 4 to 6 columns a side, out to 60–120 m. Height comes down from road level to a flat ground plane below the track's lowest point, plus noise, giving embankments and hills. Shorten a skirt where another part of the circuit is close (a coarse 20 m distance check at build time). Terrain colours in bands and blotches.\n- **Barriers** at `±(width/2 + wallL/R)`: armco, tyre stacks, concrete, or catch fence per track, with `red`/`white` paint where the corner is hard.\n- **Braking boards** at 300, 200, 100 m before major corners.\n- **Skid marks:** a ring buffer of dark quads laid where `c.sliding || c.locked`.\n- **Merging:** `mergeStaticMeshes` for the static world, as the guide says. Beyond that, performance is out of scope, so dress generously: crowds, flags, props, lights.\n\n### Track dressing\n\nEach has its own look and landmark. Build them from primitives and the library. These are the dressing lists:\n\n| Track | Hour | Look | Landmarks and props |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Kestrel Park** | 15:00 | Rolling English-style parkland, deep green, white fences | Grandstand and pit building on the straight, a windmill on the hill, oaks, hay bales, marshal posts with cloth flags, a tiny church. Kestrel bird flock (`Boids`) |\n| **Cedar Ridge** | 10:00 | Mountain forest, thin mist particles | Pines by the thousand, rock cuts, a tunnel on the rise after C6 (box arch with lamps; darkens the sky while inside), a red steel viaduct overhead, a waterfall (`WaterSurface` plus particles), a hut and hikers |\n| **Harbour Lights** | 21:00, or dusk 18:30 | Night city on the water, walls close | City blocks with lit windows (vertex colours and `glow`), neon signs in the game's own invented words, cranes and stacked containers, water along the front straight (`WaterSurface`), a Ferris wheel turning in the distance, street lamps with baked pools on the road, fireworks when the race is won |\n| **Saltline Speedbowl** | 18:30 | Endless white salt at sunset | A huge banked bowl, tall grandstand, a flag tower, a blimp circling, flat mesas on the horizon. Dust devils at distance |\n| **Red Clay Stage** | 17:00 | Orange canyon, low sun | Canyon walls (tall skirts), cacti, a rusted water tower, wooden spectator stands, arrows and warning boards before crests, dust trails that hang in the air |\n| **Proving Ground** | 11:00 | An old airfield | Control tower (Licence Centre), hangars, painted boxes, cones, a windsock (`clothGrid`), the examiner's van |\n\n### Sky and night\n\n`applySky` gives a flat background, sun and ambient. Add depth: a far ring of silhouettes at 2 to 3 km per track (hills, mesas, skyline) in two or three flat palette colours, a sun or moon disc, a few chunky clouds. For night, raise `engine.ambient.intensity` until the road reads, then let baked lamp pools, `glow` windows and the headlight do the work. Raw `skyAt(21)` with no lights is close to black.\n\n### Diorama\n\nThe Map is a chunky island about 60×40 m, with a small road loop and tiny cars (the car models at 0.25 scale) driving around it. Buildings: Licence Centre (tower), Event Hall (mini grandstand), Dealer Row (three glass showrooms), Tuning Street (garage with sparks), Service Bay (spinning brushes), Home Garage (house with a roll-up door), Museum (columns), Theatre (marquee with lights), Arcade Gate (neon arch). Boats, gulls, clouds. **The sky slowly moves with your progress:** morning at the start, dusk by the National licence, night lights for the Grand Prix. Show the current car parked at Home.\n\n### Particles\n\nRegister in code, sizes whole art pixels, palette colours: tyre smoke (`mist`), dust (`sand`, `orange`), gravel spray, sparks (`sand`, `orange`), rain spray, exhaust pop (`orange`, `sand`), car-wash foam, confetti, fireworks. Taillight `Trail`s at night.\n\n### Screen flow\n\nTransitions with `engine.screen.cover('wipe' or 'blinds')` and `reveal`. `engine.screen.flash` on the green light. `engine.hitstop(0.05)` on a very hard wall hit only.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Animation\n\n**Base:** the engine's rig, `HERO_MODEL`, `HERO_RIG`, `ClipDef`, `compileClips`, and procedural tools (`Timeline`, `tweens`, `Squash`, `clothGrid`). Existing hero clips include `Idle`, `IdleLook`, `Walk`, `Run`, `Wave`, `Sit`, `SideFlip`. Recolour the hero by swapping mesh materials by name for overalls and race suits. Check new clips with `analyzeClip`.\n\n**New clips to author as `ClipDef` data:**\n\n- `PitJack`: crouch and pump a jack lever.\n- `PitWheelGun`: kneel, gun to the hub, pull a wheel, fit a wheel, stand.\n- `PitFuel`: hold a fuel hose to the flank.\n- `MarshalFlag`: wave a flag overhead (`Wave` upper body, with the flag mesh parented to the hand).\n- `ExaminerClipboard`: note something, look up, nod.\n- `DealerPresent`: an open-handed sweep toward the car.\n- `MechanicWipe`: wipe hands on a rag, look at the car.\n- `PodiumCheer`: arms up, small jump; `PodiumBow`: a formal bow.\n- `CrowdCheer`: a looping bob for instanced stand-ins.\n\n**Procedural and mechanical motion:**\n\n- Car body pitch, roll, heave; wheels steer and spin; engine shake at idle and on the limiter; exhaust pop.\n- Turntable and slow orbit camera; a car sinking on its springs when \"bought\".\n- **Dyno:** the car on rollers, wheels spinning, curve drawn live, exhaust shake.\n- **Pit stop:** the crew runs in, jack lifts the car 0.15 m with a tween, wheels swap, crew runs off.\n- **Car wash:** spinning brushes that travel the car, foam particles, mud dissolving.\n- Start gantry lights, flags as `clothGrid`, the windsock, the windmill, the Ferris wheel, the blimp.\n- **Podium:** a `Timeline` scene (shots, cues, subtitles), confetti, the winner's car rolling in.\n- **Prize reveal:** dark set, one lamp comes on, then another, the car under a cover that slides away with a `Timeline`, fanfare, voice line.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Audio\n\nEverything is code. No audio files. All sound goes through one mix module.\n\n**Music volume defaults to 10%** on first launch and in every hub round (engine fact 3). Test it. The Settings slider moves in 5% steps. Engine, tyres and effects sit well above it.\n\n### Engine sound (the most important sound in the game)\n\n`src/game/audio/enginesynth.js` is a Web Audio graph built on any `BaseAudioContext`, so a test can render it offline:\n\n- Firing frequency `f0 = rpm / 60 × cylinders / 2`. Saw at `f0`, square at `f0 / 2`, triangle at `2 × f0`, filtered noise for exhaust, through a low-pass whose cutoff rises with throttle and rpm.\n- Gain `0.35 + 0.65 × throttle`. Rev-limiter stutter at redline. Overrun crackle on lift with the sport exhaust.\n- Per-car data: cylinders (3 for the Pico, 4, 6, V8 for the Longhorn, V10 for the Corsa, V12 for the Type 9), filter character, exhaust loudness.\n- Turbo cars: a sine whistle rising with `spool`, and a filtered noise puff on lift.\n- Connect to `audio.context.destination` once `audio.unlocked`, scaled by `audio.volume.master × sfx` every frame, respecting `muted`. Node has no context: guard it, and let tests read the synth's last rpm and throttle.\n\n### Other cars and the world\n\nOther cars: up to 3 nearest get `ctx.audio.loop` voices with `spatialize` and `pitch` from rpm. Also: tyre squeal (band-limited noise, gain from `use` and `sliding`), gravel and dirt rumble, kerb rumble (a square buzz at a rate from speed), wind (noise, gain from speed²), gear clunk, wall and car thumps (`layers`), brake lock chirp, handbrake ratchet, crowd swell near stands, rain, the pit crew's wheel gun, the lap chime, start lights beeps, and the final flag.\n\n**Menu sounds:** tick (focus), confirm, back, denied, the cash register for money, a soft camera whirr in showrooms.\n\n### Music\n\nEight original pieces as `Song` data (`bpm`, `tracks`, `patterns`, `order`), `square`, `triangle`, `saw`, `sine`, `noise` only. Write new melodies; never imitate the source's music. Style: warm, jazzy, unhurried in menus; propulsive in races. Use seventh and ninth chords in arpeggios or comping, syncopation, a walking bass, brushed noise drums.\n\n| Piece | Where | Feel | BPM | Key and harmony |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Lounge | Title, Map | Bossa lounge, soft sine lead | 92 | D♭: ii7–V7–Imaj7–vi7 with a turnaround |\n| Catalogue | Dealer Row, Museum | Vibraphone-like (sine, vibrato), very slow | 76 | Fmaj7–Em7–Dm7–G7 |\n| Socket Set | Tuning, Service | Light funk, muted square | 108 | A minor pentatonic over Am7–D9 |\n| Examination | Licence Centre | Sparse, metronomic, polite | 120 | C: bass pulse, three-note motif |\n| Apex | Day races | Driving chiptune rock | 156 | E minor: i–VI–III–VII, a lead hook |\n| City Lights | Night races | City-pop groove | 118 | B♭: Imaj7–iii7–vi7–IV |\n| Red Clay | Rally | Twangy, galloping | 132 | G mixolydian |\n| The Long Drive | Replay, credits | Calm arpeggios that open out | 84 | D: Imaj7–V/vi–vi7–IVmaj7 |\n\nPlus a short fanfare for medals and wins. Races start with music on by default, and there's a Settings option to drive with no music.\n\n### Voices\n\nAnimalese-style gibberish, written in code. `src/game/audio/voice.js`, `say(text, speaker)`: each letter of the text becomes a syllable of 65 to 85 ms, vowels pick formants through two band-pass filters over a saw (a 800/1150, e 400/2000, i 300/2400, o 450/800, u 325/700 Hz), consonants add a 12 ms noise click, the base pitch comes from the speaker, the sentence end falls, and `?` rises. A Voices slider in Settings. Subtitles always show the line in full. The same words always make the same sound, so characters seem to really be saying it.\n\n| Speaker | Pitch | Manner |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ms. Arai, chief examiner | 210 Hz | Even, unhurried |\n| Benno, mechanic | 130 Hz, square | Gravelly, short |\n| Mr. Fenn, dealer | 180 Hz | A little lilt at line ends |\n| Dell, race commentator | 160 Hz, faster | Excited, on replays and results |\n\n---\n\n## 11. Writing and cast\n\n**Voice.** The source's writing is a catalogue written by people in love with the subject: sincere, exact, formally polite, a touch translated, and funny only by being deadpan. Numbers appear with respect. Sentences are short. Nothing is \"awesome\". Nobody calls the player a champion. A car is never \"sick\". Keep to that here. All on-screen text is uppercase in the HUD font, so write it to be read that way.\n\n**Cast.** Only these four, each with a few lines per location. **Ms. Arai** at the Licence Centre has examined everyone on the Peninsula, and has never raised her voice. **Benno** at Tuning Street and the Service Bay and in the pits believes every car is one oil change from happiness. **Mr. Fenn** at Dealer Row can recite every car's kerb weight and still gets emotional. **Dell** does commentary: gibberish over replays, with subtitles of plain facts. They appear as the hero model, recoloured, in their set. No plot. The story is your car.\n\n### Brochure text\n\n| Car | Text |\n| --- | --- |\n| Pico | A city car of 660 cc, made to fit where other cars can only wish to go. At 720 kg it has little to hold back and little to push. It teaches the first lesson: momentum is expensive. |\n| Kite | The Kite was a family hatchback until the engineers stayed late. The 1.6-litre engine asks to be revved, and the front tyres do all of the work. Brake before the corner and it will thank you. |\n| Ginga | A front-engined, rear-drive coupe in good used condition. The steering speaks clearly and the rear axle answers the right foot. Many drivers learned to drive in this car. Some learned twice. |\n| Moth | Two seats, one purpose, 860 kg. Castellan removed what the car did not need, then removed a little more. The power figure is modest. That is not the figure that matters. |\n| Ryu | Turbocharged, four-wheel drive, born for a rally rule that no longer exists. Power arrives late and then all at once. In the rain, very little can keep it in sight. |\n| Longhorn | A large American coupe of the old school: seven litres, 420 PS, and brakes that remember a simpler time. It does not like corners, and it says so. On a long straight nobody asks it to apologise. |\n| Brandt 3.2 | A four-door sedan, according to the catalogue. The straight six reaches 7,900 rpm with an even temper, and the balance is close to 50:50. Businessmen bought it for the leather. Others did not. |\n| Heck | The engine sits behind the rear axle, where Brandt has insisted for forty years that it belongs. Traction is wonderful. Lifting in mid-corner is not recommended. This is written in the manual. |\n| Libeccio | Named for the south-west wind. A mid-engined V8 with a low nose and a high opinion of itself. Light on the front wheels at speed. Precise, if you are. |\n| Kite Cup | A stripped Kite for a one-make series, with a roll cage where the rear seats used to be. 210 PS through the front wheels asks for a patient foot. |\n| Ryu Rally | Works gravel specification: long travel, short gearing, and a bonnet that has seen weather. On dirt it is another species. On tarmac it seems to be looking for the exit. |\n| Works GT | The Ginga as the factory would like to remember it. 560 PS, carbon panels, and a wing with its own opinion about corner speed. |\n| Corsa | Homologated in the strictest sense of the word: Vespucci built twenty-five road cars so that it could race this one. 620 PS. 1,100 kg. Do not lift. |\n| Type 9 | Built by a small works team on the Peninsula, in a shed, for one race. 780 PS, 930 kg, a closed cockpit and no radio. It stops as well as it goes, which takes some time to believe. |\n\n### Lines to set the tone\n\n- **Ms. Arai, first meeting:** \"Welcome to the Peninsula. A licence is not difficult to obtain. It is only difficult to deserve.\"\n- **Ms. Arai, C-1 brief:** \"Accelerate to the marker, then bring the car to rest inside the white box. Please remember: the brake is also a steering wheel.\"\n- **Ms. Arai, fail:** \"The box has not moved. Please try again.\"\n- **Ms. Arai, gold:** \"Thank you. That was correct.\"\n- **Mr. Fenn, on the Kite:** \"A previous owner. One careful weekend. She has 88,000 kilometres and no regrets.\"\n- **Benno, oil:** \"Your oil is tired. It has seen things. Two hundred and fifty credits.\"\n- **Benno, new wing:** \"You now have more downforce. You also have more drag. There is no third thing.\"\n- **Dell on the results card:** \"Lap record at Kestrel Park. One minute, fifty-one. The windmill did not turn to look.\"\n- **Loading tips:** \"BRAKE IN A STRAIGHT LINE. A TYRE HAS ONLY SO MUCH GRIP TO SHARE.\" \"SLOW IN, FAST OUT. THIS HAS BEEN TRUE FOR SOME TIME.\" \"A WASHED CAR IS NOT FASTER. IT IS SIMPLY POLITE.\"\n- **Map blurbs:** Licence Centre, \"EVERY DRIVER BEGINS WITH A CLIPBOARD.\" Dealer Row, \"THREE SHOWROOMS AND A USED LOT. BRING MONEY.\" Tuning Street, \"WHAT YOU BUY HERE YOU WILL FEEL.\" Service Bay, \"OIL AND SOAP.\" Home Garage, \"EVERYTHING YOU HAVE EARNED.\" Event Hall, \"THE ROAD BETWEEN YOU AND THE GRAND PRIX.\" Museum, \"CARS YOU HAVE MET.\" Theatre, \"WATCH IT AGAIN, MORE SLOWLY.\" Arcade Gate, \"NO LICENCE REQUIRED. NO MONEY EITHER.\"\n\nWrite the rest in this voice: all test briefs and tips, event introductions, and part descriptions in one plain line each.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Attract mode\n\nAfter **10 s without input** on the title or the Map, start `src/game/attract/director.js`. Any input (key, click, pad, tap) stops it at once and returns to where the player was. It never runs in a hub session. It may cheat freely. It loops:\n\n| # | Clip | Shows | Seconds |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Title card, `DEMO`, music | Identity | 3 |\n| 2 | Showroom: three cars on the turntable, brochure text, Mr. Fenn | Cars, catalogue voice | 10 |\n| 3 | Tuning: dyno pull with before and after curve, new wing appears | Tuning | 8 |\n| 4 | Licence B-1 demonstration, medal awarded | Licences | 10 |\n| 5 | Kestrel Park: AI race, starts in chase, cuts to TV cameras, a pass | Racing, replay cameras | 20 |\n| 6 | Red Clay: the Ryu over a crest, dust, a handbrake hairpin | Rally | 12 |\n| 7 | Harbour Lights at night, then a pit stop with the crew | Night, endurance | 20 |\n| 8 | Saltline: slipstream at 300 km/h past the blimp | Speed | 10 |\n| 9 | Podium, prize car reveal | Rewards | 8 |\n| 10 | Credits card: `PRESS ANY BUTTON` | Call to action | 4 |\n\nEach race clip is a real `RaceScene` with AI on every car, `env` god settings, and hard cuts to skip dull parts. Since the player's profile isn't touched, nothing here can change the save. On a touch-only device it runs the same way.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Dev mode\n","numLines":400,"startLine":260,"totalLines":1337}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JiUeoK9E8DRi9y5CGafqPT","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
